WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:19.523 --> 00:00:24.083
We’re starting this episode out with a question&nbsp;
that we’re never going to have a good answer for:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.711 --> 00:00:30.320
how many cells do animals have? I can already&nbsp;
hear some of you scoffing some version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.209
“why is Hank Green asking me such&nbsp;
an obviously un-answerable question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:39.440
while a rotifer chills on my screen?”
And you’re not wrong to be skeptical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.120
There are a lot of animals in the world,&nbsp;
and a lot of cells inside those animals.&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:51.604
How could we ever hope to count all of the cells in&nbsp;
each of those animals? And how could we even begin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.400
to assume that the amount of cells in&nbsp;
one individual is going to be the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.480
for all the other individuals?
And what would even be the point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.920
of treating every animal like a jar full of&nbsp;
jellybeans that we need to guess the number of?&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.720
Are you waiting for the twist? Cause&nbsp;
you know there is going to be a twist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.560
Maybe you even know what the twist is already.&nbsp;
Maybe you remember our video on rotifers all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.384
the way back in 2019, where we said this:

00:01:20.953 --> 00:01:24.160
rotifers are eutelic, which means that from

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:31.040
the moment they are born, they will have a fixed&nbsp;
number of cells in their bodies for the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.767
their life. For most rotifers, that number&nbsp;
is around 1000 cells.&nbsp;

00:01:36.127 --> 00:01:38.333
And for the rotifer to grow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:43.120
the cells themselves, which&nbsp;
cannot divide, have to get bigger.&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:52.480
Think about that for a second. We know how many&nbsp;
cells are inside of a rotifer, which means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:58.160
at some point in history, someone sat down and&nbsp;
counted the cells in a rotifer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.200
And they didn’t just stop at&nbsp;
one rotifer: they kept counting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:07.920
going from rotifer to rotifer to rotifer until&nbsp;
a simple truth appeared. All of these rotifers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:15.360
had the same number of cells as each other.
So the rotifer is one of the few animals that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:23.740
we can treat like a jar full of countable
beans, and it’s because of this property called&nbsp;eutely.

00:02:25.959 --> 00:02:30.640
The number of cells in the rotifer has&nbsp;
been set, which means that if you’re looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:37.440
at a mature rotifer, you know that you’re looking&nbsp;
at about 1000 cells in action. It may be a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:43.680
more or a little less depending on the species,&nbsp;
but within those species, all the individuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:52.240
will be the same in this one very countable way.
We had hoped at this point to give you a fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:57.440
exercise in historical imagination. We’d&nbsp;
ask something like, “What kind of person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.680
would have the patience to count the cells&nbsp;
in a rotifer?” And then we’d give you the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.320
name of the scientist who did the counting, and&nbsp;
maybe even quote their writings on the subject.&nbsp;

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:10.720
However, science has many dark&nbsp;
pasts that weave through history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.840
binding together our understanding of even&nbsp;
the smallest creatures with the political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.854
and social forces that shape humanity.

00:03:21.259 --> 00:03:24.000
The word “eutely” was brought into the biological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.840
vernacular because of a zoologist named Erich&nbsp;
Martini, who in the beginning of the 20th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:36.000
counted the cells in the rotifer species Hydatina&nbsp;
senta and established the female individual&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.320
had 958 somatic cells, meaning all of the&nbsp;
cells apart from the individual’s eggs.&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.520
However, we have to add that in addition to&nbsp;
Martini’s contributions to our understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.240
of eutely, his legacy also involves a&nbsp;
debate between historians over whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:59.360
he engineered a Nazi plot to spread malaria&nbsp;
among Italian citizens in 1943, which would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:05.520
be considered an act of biological warfare.
Martini was not the first nor was he the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:11.280
last person to be associated with both&nbsp;
important scientific discovery and Nazism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.800
And while we could explore the question of&nbsp;
how many cells are inside of animals without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.440
making reference to him, we think it’s&nbsp;
important to not gloss over the ugliness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:28.000
that often accompanies the history of science.
Other scientists were also fascinated by eutely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:34.320
In 1932, the scientist Harvey J. Van&nbsp;
Cleave published his paper “Eutely or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:39.760
Cell Constancy in its Relation to Body Size,”&nbsp;
confirming the cell counts that Martini had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:45.120
made in his rotifer studies while also describing&nbsp;
the evidence researchers had found for eutely in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:51.960
animals like gastrotrichs and nematodes. And other&nbsp;
scientists would add tardigrades to the list.&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:05:00.720
Every animal begins its life as a single cell&nbsp;
that is full of destiny. It divides and divides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:07.520
and divides, sending cells tumbling down a path&nbsp;
toward their fate as they take on specific shapes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:13.040
and functions that have been preordained through&nbsp;
a combination of chemical and genetic cues.&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.280
In most animal bodies, including our own,&nbsp;
many of those cells continue to divide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:25.760
whether that’s to help us grow or heal or adapt.&nbsp;
So while scientists have estimated that humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:33.760
have around 37.2 trillion cells, their estimate&nbsp;
is, by necessity, based on very restrictive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:40.960
assumptions about what a human body looks like.
Ultimately, most of us will exist somewhere in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:48.160
a gray area of variation around that number. Some&nbsp;
of us will have more, some of us will have less.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.760
Whether there’s a deeper pattern underlying the&nbsp;
disparities is hard to pin down, except for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:59.920
fact that the heart of nature is diversity.
But not for the rotifer. Sure, it started with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:05.280
egg that divided and made more cells that&nbsp;
followed a set path to their final fate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:10.880
eventually growing into the mature wheeled&nbsp;
animal we love to watch. But when the rotifer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:17.920
reaches maturity, it no longer makes any new&nbsp;
cells. If it needs to grow, then the cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:24.480
will grow, but they will not make new cells.
And perhaps not needing to divide and make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:30.240
new cells creates a sort of peaceful constancy&nbsp;
that means the rotifer doesn’t need to waste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:36.080
energy on making new DNA and duplicating&nbsp;
its cells. But it also means that if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:41.120
rotifer gets hurt, it doesn’t have the&nbsp;
tools to make new cells to heal itself.&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:48.000
We don’t really have an idea of why rotifers&nbsp;
or any other animal would become eutelic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.680
There’s no obvious phylogenetic relationship that&nbsp;
exists between eutelic animals that we know of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:59.200
And at times, identifying eutely in one species&nbsp;
has led us to make incorrect assumptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:02.800
about their relatives.
That’s because eutely itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:07.200
is a diverse phenomenon. Some of those&nbsp;
animals that we mentioned were eutelic?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:13.520
It turns out they are…but they also aren’t.&nbsp;
Tardigrades, for example, have storage cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.280
that help distribute energy but that also&nbsp;
sometimes divide to make more storage cells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.080
And some gastrotrichs have even been&nbsp;
documented healing themselves after an injury.&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:30.400
These may be cases of partial eutely, where&nbsp;
some parts of the animal do maintain some kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:36.640
of constancy while others do not. Or it may&nbsp;
be that even within a phylum, the occurrence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:43.040
of eutely has no obvious logic to it—maybe some&nbsp;
gastrotrichs just are eutelic, and some aren’t.&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:50.480
And as we said, those kinds of cases just&nbsp;
add to the confusion of eutely. The nematode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:56.240
Caenorhabditis elegans has been such a popular&nbsp;
model organism in part because we know that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:04.640
adult C. elegans hermaphrodite has a body built&nbsp;
out of 959 somatic cells. And knowing the path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:12.240
from egg to embryo to juvenile to adult&nbsp;
through the story of those 959 somatic cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:18.640
has been a tremendous gift to science.&nbsp;
It’s a blueprint of the worm’s development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:24.720
a pattern that scientists can prove and&nbsp;
disrupt to uncover other hidden motifs.&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:32.000
But it turns out that C. elegans’ eutely is&nbsp;
not the shared trait it was once thought to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.320
among nematodes. Free-living marine nematodes&nbsp;
were found to not have it And other species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.840
have been found whose skin is dotted with a&nbsp;
different number of nuclei, revealing variation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:51.040
where previous scientists hadn’t expected any.
And so even in these unusual cases where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:56.400
it seems like maybe we can get closer to&nbsp;
quantifying the number of cells in an animal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:02.240
the simple act of counting paints a much&nbsp;
more complicated picture of the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:11.600
Nature loves to change, even if&nbsp;that is
a change towards a bizarre case, of constancy.&nbsp; 

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:21.840
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:26.800
And thank you to all of the people&nbsp;
whose names are on the screen right now.&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:29.840
They are our patrons.
They, in a way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:38.400
are like the individual cells that make of the&nbsp;
